        parentBriefing: "This is the parent's theological humility sitting. Augustine's cor inquietum is a Christian claim about God. It is also a phenomenological claim about human experience that a secular philosopher can engage. Your job is to teach both layers — the argument and the conclusion — without forcing the conclusion. Whether you accept the theology is your business. Whether your child can understand the argument is the academy's business.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't present Augustine's conclusion as the only option. The restlessness is real; what it points at is contested. Let your child contest it.", "If you are a secular parent, don't dismiss the argument because you reject the conclusion. The phenomenology of desire is real regardless of what you think it points at."],
        reading: [{ heading: "Teaching the argument, not the answer", body: "Augustine's Confessions: 'Our heart is restless until it rests in you.' The adult version of this sitting is about pedagogy, not philosophy. How do you teach a child that desire is epistemologically significant — that wanting something you can't name might be evidence — without telling them what the evidence proves? The answer: teach the argument, not the conclusion. The argument: human beings have a hunger that nothing in the world permanently satisfies. That hunger is not random — it is structured, persistent, and universal. Augustine says it points at God. A secular philosopher says it points at meaning, purpose, or something unnamed. The argument is the same. The conclusions differ. Your child needs to understand the argument so they can evaluate the conclusions — including yours. The parent theological humility note: this is intellectual history, not catechism. The academy teaches how Augustine reasoned. Whether you accept his conclusion is your own work — and your child's." }],
        bigIdea: "Teach the argument, not the answer. The restlessness is real. What it points at is the conversation — and your child deserves to be in it.",
