        parentBriefing: "This is the hardest sitting for parents. The problem of evil is not abstract for a child who has lost someone, experienced illness, or witnessed cruelty. Augustine's theory — evil is the absence of good — is a philosophical argument. It is not a pastoral response. Do not use philosophy to comfort a child in real pain. Use presence. The philosophy is for when the pain has a name and the child is ready to think about it.",
        hardEdges: ["Do not use the privation theory to minimize your child's suffering. 'It's just the absence of good' is not comfort — it is abstraction applied to a wound.", "This is a Christian theodicy. It is also a serious philosophical argument. If your family has experienced real loss, the argument may feel insufficient. That is a fair response. Let it be insufficient."],
        reading: [{ heading: "When philosophy meets pain", body: "Augustine's privation theory: evil is not a substance; it is the absence of good. The will chooses a lesser good over the highest, and that distortion is the origin of moral evil. The adult question: how do you teach this to a child who has experienced real suffering? The answer: carefully, and not too early. The privation theory is a philosophical framework for thinking about evil — not a pastoral response to experiencing it. If your child is in pain, they need presence, not philosophy. When the pain has a name and the child is ready to think, the framework is useful: it says evil is not a force equal to good, it is a distortion, a going-wrong. That matters because it means evil is not ultimate — it is parasitic on good. It cannot exist on its own. Whether that is comfort or cold abstraction is your judgment — and your child's. The parent theological humility note: this is one theodicy among many. Augustine's answer is not the only answer. It is the most influential one in Western thought. Understanding it is intellectual history. Living with the problem is human experience. Both matter." }],
