        title: "The Parent's City",
        dek: "Your family is a city. What does it love most? Augustine's question, applied to the smallest institution you run.",
        objective: "The adult can apply Augustine's two-cities framework to their own family and evaluate what their family loves most.",
        parentBriefing: "This sitting applies Augustine to the family. Your household is a city. It is shaped by what it loves most. If it loves achievement, it serves achievement. If it loves comfort, it serves comfort. If it loves people, it serves people. The question is honest and uncomfortable. The theological frame is optional; the political analysis is not.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't reduce this to 'family values' politics. Augustine is asking what your household actually serves — not what it says it serves.", "The gap between what you say your family loves and what it actually loves is the sitting. That gap is where the earthly city hides."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The smallest city", body: "Augustine's two cities run through every institution — including the family. Your household is a city. It has a love that shapes it. If it loves achievement above all, your children learn they are their grades. If it loves comfort, they learn that discomfort is the enemy. If it loves reputation, they learn that image matters more than truth. If it loves people — their actual flourishing, not their performance — they learn that they are worth something regardless of what they produce. The question Augustine asks of empires, you can ask of your dinner table: what does this city love most? Not what does it say it loves. What does it actually serve — shown by where the time goes, where the money goes, what gets praised, what gets punished. The parent theological humility note: this is a theological frame (love of God vs. love of self) applied to domestic life. A secular parent can translate: what does your family actually serve — and is it what you want to serve? The question is portable. The answer is yours." }],
