        parentBriefing: "One object. That sentence, and nothing beside it. Read the short reading aloud. On paper, with no account, the family says it once. English only. No new term after it. The wording is the narrower claim from Pegis's Chapter 7, as the note shows. Do not put \"Aquinas said\" in front of a wings sentence or in front of \"truth cannot contradict truth.\" Do not teach faith and reason as wings, of a bird, or of a flight. Do not put John Paul II's wings sentence on this page, as his or as Aquinas's. Do not widen the claim. The parent holds whether it is true. There is no account, no model prompt, and no Hermes card. Parent note: Not part of the reading aloud. The English of the chapter is Anton C. Pegis's, in *Contra Gentiles*, Book One, Chapter 7, \"That the truth of reason is not opposed to the truth of the Christian faith.\" The sentence on the child's page is the brief's narrower claim. It is not one sentence Pegis printed in that word order. Pegis, same chapter, paragraph [1]: \"Since, therefore, only the false is opposed to the true, as is clearly evident from an examination of their definitions, it is impossible that the truth of faith should be opposed to those principles that the human reason knows naturally.\" \"Because only the false is opposed to the true\" is this page's English for his \"Since, therefore, only the false is opposed to the true.\" His since-clause comes first. This page says the faith clause first, then the because. \"Is not opposed\" is this page's English for \"it is impossible that the truth of faith should be opposed.\" The page does not add \"as is clearly evident from an examination of their definitions.\" It does not change his sentence into \"truth cannot contradict truth.\" The same paragraph, before that, says: \"Now, although the truth of the Christian faith which we have discussed surpasses the capacity of the reason, nevertheless that truth that the human reason is naturally endowed to know cannot be opposed to the truth of the Christian faith.\" That sentence is not the line the child says. This page does not teach \"surpasses the capacity of the reason.\" It is not a second lesson, and it is not a replacement. Paragraph [2], last sentence: \"That which we hold by faith as divinely revealed, therefore, cannot be contrary to our natural knowledge.\" That sentence is the same chapter. It is not a second line on this page. It does not say \"truth cannot contradict truth.\" The Latin, same chapter, Leonine text, paragraph [23537], n. 2: \"Quia igitur solum falsum vero contrarium est, ut ex eorum definitionibus inspectis manifeste apparet, impossibile est illis principiis quae ratio naturaliter cognoscit, praedictam veritatem fidei contrariam esse.\" The chapter uses *contrarium*. It does not contain the slogan. The child's page does not ask anyone to learn the Latin. This sitting adds no new term. Paragraph [23538], n. 3, closing sentence: \"Ea igitur quae ex revelatione divina per fidem tenentur, non possunt naturali cognitioni esse contraria.\" That is the Latin of the paragraph [2] sentence. It stays in this note.",
        hardEdges: ["Do not teach two wings, a bird, or a flight for faith and reason. The opened Aquinas text does not mention that figure. Do not put the wings sentence on this page as John Paul II's or as Aquinas's. Do not say \"of the same bird\" or \"of a single flight.\" Do not put those phrases back in other words.", "Do not teach \"truth cannot contradict truth\" as his sentence. Do not say \"contradict\" for this claim. Pegis's word is \"opposed.\"", "Do not widen the claim. Do not add \"surpasses the capacity of the reason,\" a conflict to solve, a case, or a second book.", "The parent holds the question. Do not ask a model. No account."],
