        parentBriefing: "Read the short reading aloud. One object: the hazelnut. One difference, in English before the names. \"Everything is fine\" is optimism. \"The worst is not the end\" is hope. Julian's sentence, said during the Black Death, is the second. This is not a catechism. The parent holds whether the claim is true. There is no account, no model prompt, and no Hermes card. Do not restore the line that Julian is the first woman to write a book in English. No source for it was opened. Do not put another historical claim in its place. Parent note, not part of the student reading: The words in the reading are carried from the emerging sitting. Revelations of Divine Love was not opened. No scholarly source was opened. This draft does not say the book supports, contradicts, or omits those sentences. The sitting calls her a mystic who argues from a vision, not from premises. That label was not checked. Use it only so the page stays one vision and one difference, not a system. The parent holds whether the claim is true. A family does not have to accept the theology to learn the difference.",
        hardEdges: ["Do not say Julian is the first woman to write a book in English. Do not replace that sentence.", "Do not teach whether sin is necessary, and do not say sin is good. That is a second lesson.", "Do not say everything is fine, and do not use \"all shall be well\" to dismiss a hard thing the family names.", "Do not add a date, a death toll, a war, or a famine. The kept context is the Black Death, as the sitting already says it.", "The parent holds the question. Do not ask a model."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The hazelnut, then the difference", body: "Julian of Norwich saw something the size of a hazelnut in her hand. She asked what it was. The answer she gives is: it is all that is made. God made it. God loves it. God keeps it. She said: all shall be well, and all shall be well, and all manner of thing shall be well. She said this during the Black Death. \"Everything is fine\" is one sentence. \"The worst is not the end\" is a different sentence. The first is optimism. The second is hope. She is claiming the second." }],
        bigIdea: "Hope is not optimism. \"All shall be well\" means the worst is not the end, not that everything is fine.",
