        parentBriefing: "The parent theological humility note: the Trinity is specifically Christian doctrine. A non-Christian student can engage it as metaphysics — the claim that reality is fundamentally relational. A secular philosopher can ask: is relationship prior to substance? The Cappadocians say yes. Whether you accept their theological framing, the philosophical question is real.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't force the theology. The philosophical question — is relationship the structure of reality? — is available without the Trinity.", "If your family is struggling relationally, the claim that 'relationship is the structure of reality' may feel either comforting or impossible. Let it be either."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The family as Trinity", body: "The Cappadocian claim: God is one essence, three persons — and 'person' means 'mode of existence,' not 'individual.' The philosophical implication: reality is fundamentally relational. Relationship is not an add-on to substance; it is the substance. The adult version: what does this mean for your family? If relationship is the structure of reality, then your family is not a collection of individuals who happen to be together. It is a set of relationships that constitutes the individuals. You are not a parent who has relationships. You are a parent because of relationships. Your child is not a person who has a family. They are a person constituted by family. This is a radical claim. It says the relationships are more real than the individuals — because the individuals only exist as persons within the relationships. Whether you accept the Trinity, the philosophical claim is live: are we substances that enter relationships, or are we relationships that crystallize as substances? The Cappadocians say the latter. Your family is the evidence." }],
