        parentBriefing: "This is intellectual history, not catechism. Gregory's framework: the active life is necessary, the contemplative life is superior, but the integrated life — action flowing from contemplation and returning to it — is the goal. The AI connection: the student who asks the model for answers without reflecting is all action, no contemplation. The student who reads philosophy without applying it is all contemplation, no action.",
        hardEdges: ["Contemplation is not 'doing nothing.' Gregory says it is the hardest work — facing reality without distraction.", "Don't present this as 'monastic spirituality.' Gregory was Pope — he administered hospitals, negotiated with invaders, managed a diocese. His argument is for people with jobs, not for monks."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The integrated life", body: "Gregory the Great's framework: the active life (serving in the world) and the contemplative life (seeking truth in silence) are not opposites. The active life is necessary — faith without works is dead. The contemplative life is superior — Mary chose the better part. But the integrated life is the goal: action flowing from contemplation and returning to it. You serve because you have reflected. You reflect because you have served. The movement between them is the discipline. The AI connection: the student who asks the model for answers without reflecting is all action, no contemplation — reactive, ungrounded. The student who reads philosophy without applying it is all contemplation, no action — abstract, inert. The sitting asks: where is your balance? The integrity rule: do not let the model replace either the thinking or the doing. The model can help with both. It cannot be either." }],
        bigIdea: "Thinking and doing are not rivals. The integrated life moves between them — action grounded in reflection, reflection tested in action.",
        tryThis: [{ title: "Audit your balance", minutes: 16, steps: ["Name one way you are all action (reacting, doing, responding) without reflection.", "Name one way you are all contemplation (thinking, reading, planning) without action.", "Where is the balance? What would 'action flowing from reflection and returning to it' look like?", "Practice/Reflect: is the model replacing your contemplation (you ask it instead of thinking) or your action (you read its output instead of doing)?"] }],
        dinnerQuestion: "Where are we all doing and no thinking — or all thinking and no doing?",
        transfer: [{ label: "AI", note: "The model is the temptation to skip contemplation (just ask) and skip action (just read). The integrated life requires both." }, { label: "Stoics", note: "Gregory's active-contemplative balance echoes Marcus Aurelius — the emperor who journaled before acting. Same structure, different theology." }],
        ifTheySay: [
          { heard: "Contemplation sounds like doing nothing.", reply: "Gregory would say contemplation is the hardest work — it is facing reality without distraction. Most people can serve the poor for an hour. Few can sit in silence for an hour and face what they actually believe. The active life feels productive; the contemplative life is productive in a way that does not feel like it." },
          { heard: "I'm a practical person — I don't need contemplation.", reply: "Gregory's answer: the active life without contemplation becomes reactive — you respond to every crisis without asking whether your response serves the good or just relieves your anxiety. Contemplation is what keeps action honest. Without it, you are not serving; you are managing your discomfort." },
        ],
